<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Magadhi Prakrit</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Magadhi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> was the Middle Indo-Aryan vernacular of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It is associated with the speech of the Buddha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a>, appears in the eastern versions of the inscriptions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>, and is used for some lower-class characters in classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> drama. The eastern Indo-Aryan languages, including Bengali, Odia, Assamese and the Bihari languages, descend from its later forms.</p></div>
